virtio_pci: poll the reset of a modern device at a short interval

vp_reset() waited for the status to read 0 with msleep(1), which sleeps
for 2 ms at HZ=1000. On Virtualization.framework the first read after
the write of 0 is not 0, so each of the three virtio PCI devices of
the ztcl guest took 2.0 ms to probe. The reset now polls with
usleep_range(10, 20) for up to 100 tries, then falls back to
msleep(1).

Measured on Virtualization.framework (M1, 1 CPU, 128 MB, initcall_debug):
virtio_pci_driver_init took 6.16 ms before, with 2.15, 2.01, and
2.00 ms for the probes of the three devices, and 3.10 ms after. Guest
boot to agent listen, 10 boots each: best 9.81 and median 10.02 ms
before, best 6.72 and median 7.01 ms after.

--- a/drivers/virtio/virtio_pci_modern.c
+++ b/drivers/virtio/virtio_pci_modern.c
@@ -434,6 +434,7 @@
 {
 	struct virtio_pci_device *vp_dev = to_vp_device(vdev);
 	struct virtio_pci_modern_device *mdev = &vp_dev->mdev;
+	unsigned int i;
 
 	/* 0 status means a reset. */
 	vp_modern_set_status(mdev, 0);
@@ -441,9 +442,17 @@
 	 * device_status to return 0 before reinitializing the device.
 	 * This will flush out the status write, and flush in device writes,
 	 * including MSI-X interrupts, if any.
+	 *
+	 * The device of a VMM can end the reset a few microseconds after the
+	 * write. msleep(1) sleeps for 2 ms at HZ=1000, so poll at a short
+	 * interval first, for up to about 2 ms, then fall back to msleep(1).
 	 */
-	while (vp_modern_get_status(mdev))
-		msleep(1);
+	for (i = 0; vp_modern_get_status(mdev); i++) {
+		if (i < 100)
+			usleep_range(10, 20);
+		else
+			msleep(1);
+	}
 
 	/* Flush pending VQ/configuration callbacks. */
 	vp_synchronize_vectors(vdev);
